  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B004",
        "laureates": [
          "Christopher Jarzynski",
          "Denis J. Evans",
          "Gavin E. Crooks"
        ],
        "citation": "for exact fluctuation theorems and nonequilibrium work relations"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B024",
    "B035"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. B004 identifies a clean, universal mechanism in nonequilibrium statistical physics: exact relations connect irreversible fluctuations and work measurements to equilibrium free-energy differences beyond the near-equilibrium regime. Evans, Jarzynski, and Crooks receive differentiated and compact credit for foundational fluctuation-theorem work, the work equality, and the general fluctuation formulation. This is a mature conceptual achievement, rigorously understood and repeatedly tested in small systems, that changed the physical treatment of fluctuations far from equilibrium.",
    "case_against": "The principal comparative weakness remains that B004 has less concentrated instrumental, technological, or clinical impact than leading alternatives. Its historical boundary must also be checked carefully, both against precursors to the fluctuation theorems and against the broad later experimental literature, so that the citation rewards the exact relations themselves rather than an extended field.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson judges B024 a mature bridge between many-body theory and real materials, but regards its methodological development and attribution as distributed.",
        "response": "I agree with both parts of that assessment. B024 is still my leading theoretical alternative because it made correlated-electron calculations quantitatively productive. But the distributed development of a broadly evolving method makes a three-person allocation less secure than for B004, whose three credited names map directly onto closely specified exact relations."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth argues that B035 has exceptionally clear complementary credit, independent replication, and mature precision measurements, without needing to rely on prospective applications.",
        "response": "This strengthens B035 as my leading experimental alternative. The independent realization of optical lattice clocks is a particularly persuasive maturity test. I nevertheless retain B004 because its exact relations supplied a general physical principle across nonequilibrium systems; the clocks are a superbly realized measurement architecture, but their citation boundary still requires separation from the wider collective development of optical frequency metrology."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll emphasizes that B032 is a mature, experimentally decisive imaging invention, while requiring a strict distinction between the original OCT invention and later collective development.",
        "response": "I agree that OCT is an outstanding example of a durable physical instrument and that its demonstrated consequences are stronger than those of many conceptual proposals. The same boundary discipline, however, favors B004: its narrowly identifiable theorems, rather than subsequent applications and tests, provide a more self-contained fundamental achievement with compact credit."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the historical record fully supports assigning the foundational fluctuation-theorem, work-equality, and general-relation contributions to precisely these three names.",
      "Whether the comparative weight of a universal conceptual advance should exceed the more immediately visible mature instrumental and metrological achievements represented by B032 and B035."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No specific conflict is apparent. My profile notes a shared Gothenburg physics environment with Göran Johansson and Eva Olsson, but identifies no collaboration with either."
  }
}
